Package: raiseR
Type: Package
Title: Ordinary and Robust Raise, Ridge, and Liu Regression, Condition
        Number, and Variance Inflation Factors
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: person(given = "Jinse", family = "Jacob", email = "jinsejacob@hotmail.com",
    role = c("aut", "cre"), comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-0873-4864"))
Description: Implements the Raise Method as an inference-preserving alternative to
    Ridge Regression for combating multicollinearity in linear models, including the
    classical single-variable Raise Method, the Simultaneous Raise Regression (SRR)
    based on QR decomposition and the Sequential Variance Inflation Factor (SVIF)
    of Jacob and Varadharajan (2022) <doi:10.1007/s11135-022-01557-9>, and the
    original raise parameter selection strategy of Jacob and Varadharajan (2023)
    <doi:10.13189/ms.2023.110106>. Also implements a Robust Raise Method for data
    contaminated by outliers, with exact finite-sample inference (sandwich standard
    errors, Wald tests, Satterthwaite-corrected degrees of freedom) obtained by
    down-weighting observations using Stahel-Donoho projection outlyingness and
    Tukey's biweight function. Provides ordinary and robust Ridge Regression (Hoerl
    and Kennard, 1970, Technometrics, 12(1), 55-67), ordinary and robust Liu
    Regression (Liu, 1993, Communications in Statistics - Theory and Methods,
    22(2), 393-402), with the robust MM variants of both following the MM-estimator
    framework of Yohai (1987, Annals of Statistics, 15(2), 642-656) and, for Liu
    regression specifically, the biasing-parameter derivation of Filzmoser and
    Kurnaz's robust MM-Liu estimator. Also provides the classical Variance
    Inflation Factor (VIF) and Condition Number (Belsley, 1991) computed from the
    correlation matrix of the predictors, and the Robust Variance Inflation Factor
    (RVIF) and robust Condition Number of Jacob and Varadharajan (2024, Sankhya B,
    86(2), 845-871), which use the same projection outlyingness and biweight
    downweighting scheme to obtain a weighted correlation matrix that resists the
    influence of outliers. A flexible scale() function supports classical
    (mean/sd), robust weighted (Stahel-Donoho/Tukey biweight), median/MADN, and
    min-max [0, 1] scaling. Diagnostic and goodness-of-fit plots, and the standard
    influence-diagnostic suite (Cook's distance, DFBETAS, COVRATIO) and
    heteroskedasticity tests (via the 'lmtest' and 'car' packages) analogous to
    those for objects of class 'lm', are provided for the (exact, unbiased) Raise
    Method fit.
